# Copy to .env.local for local development (that file is git-ignored).
# On the live site, set the same names as secrets / environment variables on the host. Never commit real values.
# These are SERVER-ONLY: never give them a VITE_ prefix, which would publish them in the website's code.

# The website's AI assistant ("Gia") calls OpenAI from the server. Without this key the chat button
# still appears, but visitors see a short "not available, call or WhatsApp us" message.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Optional. The model that answers. Default: gpt-4.1. gpt-4.1-mini is about 5x cheaper but follows
# the instructions less closely (it often leaves out page links).
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4.1

# Optional. The small model that decides whether a message is about loans or GCS before the main model
# is asked (anything else is turned away, and three such messages pause the chat). Default: gpt-4.1-nano.
# A message it calls off topic is double-checked by gpt-4.1-mini before it counts.
# OPENAI_GUARD_MODEL=gpt-4.1-nano

# Optional. Where the server reaches the CRM to read live rates, contact details and wording.
# Only needed if the website's own /crm/api path is not reachable from the server (a Cloudflare Worker
# often cannot call its own public domain). Example: https://crm.example.com/crm/api
# CRM_INTERNAL_URL=

# The chat asks visitors to confirm their mobile number with a code before it opens. The CRM sends
# and checks the code (see its SMS_PROVIDER settings), so the website needs nothing more than a
# reachable CRM: if the CRM can't be asked, the chat shows its "not available, call or WhatsApp us"
# message rather than letting anyone in.
